  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1... \nA 500MB minor shortwave trough is expected to cross the area \ntoday as the cold front that has been draped from west to east\nacross the area. Still a slight west-northwest flow is expected\nthrough the day. This is expected to push the cold front through\nthe area by 00Z tonight. Ahead of the front, additional shower\nand thunderstorm activity is expected to develop today. A still\nrather moist environment is in place. \n\nPWATs are expected to be 1.8 to 2.0 through the day today and\nsome spots could be up to 2.1 PWATs, giving an idea how much\nmoisture is in the atmosphere for today. NBM probabilities tend\nto be a bit low on the rainfall expectations but the HREF gave a\n24 hour rainfall prob of exceeding 0.50\" at around 30% to 50%.\nThus, despite the saturated atmosphere, the moisture seems to \nbe under utilized. Still some 1000 to 1500 SB CAPE is possible \ntoday and enough to expect some instances of thunderstorms with \nenough instability to work with, to produce some heavy rainfall.\nThe bulk of the instability being mainly in southeast Ohio. \nThus, expect at least some isolated instances of flooding and a \nnon-zero chance for a strong storm or two. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...\nSurface high pressure within brief NW flow supports at least 1\nday of dry weather as temperature drops below the 80s (but\nremains above normal). But the longwave pattern is favored by\nensemble models to position upper ridging over the Tennessee to\nlower Mississippi River Valleys while driving a series of \nshortwaves over the ridge top through the Ohio River Valley. The\nresult is more waves of showers and thunderstorms through early\nnext week that, pending the localized storm environment for a \ngiven day, could contain various hazard threats.\n\nThe most likely hazardous risk over this time frame is flash\nflood given guidance maintains mean PWAT values between 1.4\" to\n1.9\" inches (between the 90th to above daily max, respectively).\nThis risk could be further exacerbated if the region does\nexperience multiple rounds of heavy rain (even starting\ntonight/Thursday) that causes near-saturated grounds that\nsignificantly lowers flash flood guidance. It may be\nmeteorological fall, but summer-like storm risks bear monitoring\ninto early next week.",
